## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical dynamic connectivity under batch updates—a common scenario in real systems like messaging platforms (Discord, Slack) where friendship edges change in bursts. It evaluates understanding of amortized analysis, hybrid data structures, and the classic time-space trade-off in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- **Disjoint Set Union (DSU):** Provides near-constant time union-find with path compression and union by size
- **Batch processing:** Groups deletions to amortize costly operations across multiple updates
- **Threshold-based rebuilds:** Avoids accumulating O(n) drift from many deletions by periodically recomputing from scratch
- **BFS for local reconnection:** Only re-explores components affected by deletions, not the entire graph

## Code Example
```python
class DynamicComponents:
    def __init__(self, n, edges):
        self.n = n
        self.parent = list(range(n))
        self.size = [1] * n
        self.edges = set()
        for u, v in edges:
            self.add_edge(u, v)
        self.rebuild_threshold = int(n ** 0.5)

    def find(self, x):
        if self.parent[x] != x:
            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])
        return self.parent[x]

    def union(self, x, y):
        rx, ry = self.find(x), self.find(y)
        if rx == ry:
            return
        if self.size[rx] < self.size[ry]:
            rx, ry = ry, rx
        self.parent[ry] = rx
        self.size[rx] += self.size[ry]

    def add_edge(self, u, v):
        self.edges.add((min(u, v), max(u, v)))
        self.union(u, v)

    def process_batch(self, additions, deletions):
        for u, v in additions:
            self.add_edge(u, v)
        deletionsPerformed = 0
        for u, v in deletions:
            if (min(u, v), max(u, v)) in self.edges:
                self.edges.discard((min(u, v), max(u, v)))
                deletionsPerformed += 1
        if deletionsPerformed > self.rebuild_threshold:
            self.rebuild()
        return self.count_components()

    def rebuild(self):
        self.parent = list(range(self.n))
        self.size = [1] * self.n
        for u, v in self.edges:
            self.union(u, v)

    def count_components(self):
        return sum(1 for i in range(self.n) if self.find(i) == i)
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis for dynamic frequency tracking, demonstrates how to monitor hot keys efficiently, and contrasts incremental updates with full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized analysis for batched updates
- Frequency buckets for O(1) max-frequency queries
- Hash maps for per-label counts
- Sparse vs. dense label domains
- Space-time tradeoff analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
class HotKeyTracker {
  constructor(initialCounts) {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.maxFreq = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(initialCounts)) {
      this.cnt.set(k, v);
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, v);
    }
  }

  update(label, delta) {
    const old = this.cnt.get(label) || 0;
    const freqCount = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
    if (freqCount === 1) this.freq.delete(old);
    else this.freq.set(old, freqCount - 1);

    const nw = old + delta;
    this.cnt.set(label, nw);
    this.freq.set(nw, (this.freq.get(nw) || 0) + 1);
    this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, nw);
  }

  hasHotKey(threshold) {
    return this.maxFreq >= threshold;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
A practical dynamic 2x2 submatrix counter seizes local updates; analytic challenge: bound work per update and handle edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Local neighborhood counting; incremental updates
- Amortized O(B) per batch; worst-case O(B) with 4 checks per update
- Space: store full matrix and current count; avoid full scan

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateCell(mat, n, i, j, val, cur) {
  const old = mat[i][j];
  if (old === val) return cur;
  for (let di = -1; di <= 0; di++) {
    for (let dj = -1; dj <= 0; dj++) {
      const r = i + di, c = j + dj;
      if (r >= 0 && c >= 0 && r + 1 < n && c + 1 < n) {
        const before = mat[r][c] & mat[r+1][c] & mat[r][c+1] & mat[r+1][c+1];
        // after flip not yet applied
        // compute new values after applying val to mat[i][j]
        const a = (r === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r][c];
        const b = (r+1 === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c];
        const c2 = (r === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r][c+1];
        const d = (r+1 === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c+1];
        const after = a & b & c2 & d;
        if (before !== after) {
          cur += (after ? 1 : -1) * 1; // adjust
        }
      }
    }
  }
  mat[i][j] = val;
  return cur;
}
```

