## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming distinct counting under memory constraints, a common real-world need in chat pipelines. Introduces HyperLogLog beyond basic frequency sketches and checks ability to reason about merging across shards.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Memory-constant sketch with m registers and 5–6 bits per register
- Per-element update and merge operations (max per register)
- Relative error ~1.04/√m; small-bias corrections for tiny cardinalities
- Trade-offs: m vs accuracy vs memory; cross-shard merging

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateHLL(M, xHash64) {
  const p = M.length ? Math.log2(M.length) : 0;
  const i = xHash64 >>> (64 - p);
  const w = (xHash64 << p) >>> 0;
  const v = 1 + Math.clz32(~w); // rough trailing zeros proxy
  M[i] = Math.max(M[i], v);
}

function estimateCardinality(M) {
  const m = M.length;
  const sum = M.reduce((acc, x) => acc + Math.pow(2, -x), 0);
  const alpha = (m === 16) ? 0.673 : (m === 32) ? 0.697 : (m === 64) ? 0.709 : 0.721;
  return (alpha * m * m) / sum;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to exploit DAG structure for incremental shortest paths with monotone updates and selective recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological order
- Monotone updates and lazy propagation
- Selective relaxation focused on targets
- Amortized analysis of edge relaxations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for batch decreases
function applyDecreases(batch) {
  const queue = [];
  for (const e of batch) {
    if (dist[e.v] > dist[e.u] + e.newW) {
      dist[e.v] = dist[e.u] + e.newW;
      queue.push(e.v);
    }
  }
  queue.sort((a,b)=>topo[a]-topo[b]);
  while (queue.length) {
    const x = queue.shift();
    for (const y of adj[x]) {
      const w = weight(x,y);
      if (dist[y] > dist[x] + w) {
        dist[y] = dist[x] + w;
        queue.push(y);
      }
    }
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle dynamic target set changes efficiently?
- What edge cases degrade the amortized bound and how could you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design online dynamic order-statistics with batch inserts, focusing on tangible metrics (inversions) and practical data structures, not abstract theory.

## Key Concepts

- Inversions, Fenwick tree, coordinate compression, online batch processing, duplicates handling, memory trade-offs.

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
}
// assume valueToRank and rankToValue are defined, U is number of ranks
let bit = new Fenwick(U);
let totalInv = 0;
let seen = 0;
for (const x of batch){
  const r = valueToRank[x];
  totalInv += seen - bit.query(r);
  bit.update(r, 1);
  seen++;
}
```

